          "title": "You'll want to know: dēlīrō, dēlīrāre",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "dēlīrō, dēlīrāre — \"to be delirious, talk nonsense\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Aeger febrī dēlīrat. The patient is delirious with fever."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "dēlīrō, dēlīrāre, dēlīrāvī: dē 'out of' + līra 'furrow' — to plough crooked, then to wander in mind. English delirium."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "dēlīrō is built on dē- \"away from\" and līra \"a furrow\" (Lewis & Short; EtymDB): first to plough out of the line, then to wander in mind. English delirious and delirium come from it."
            }
          ]
        },
